# ============================ ACCOUNTS 3 ============================
LESSONS_A3 = [
L("Chapter 1 — The outside witness", "11", "<p>Every check so far compared the business's records with each other. This module's check is stronger, because one side of it is a record the business does not write: the <b>bank statement</b>. The reconciliation that examines it monthly is accounting's most important routine, and this module covers it end to end — the setup, the matching, the differences, cash handling, the retail cash-up chain, and bank governance.</p><p><b>Why the outside record matters.</b> Internal records can share a disease: the same wrong default poisons the invoice and the register together; the same fraud writes both sides of its own story. The bank's record cannot be reached by any of it. The statement says what money <b>actually</b> moved, when, and where. Every internal money record ultimately settles against the statement, and a business whose books reconcile to its banks monthly has anchored its whole financial story to a record nobody inside the building can edit. A business whose books do not reconcile has an unanchored story — whatever else it has.</p><p><b>What reconciliation is.</b> The disciplined comparison: every statement line matched to a GL entry, every GL bank entry matched to a statement line, and the unmatched remainder <b>named, explained, and worked</b> (Chapters 3-4). Three things it is not:</p><p>• <b>Not a formality</b> — a tick-and-file that examines nothing proves nothing.<br>• <b>Not a plug</b> — a balancing journal that forces agreement hides every real difference. Chapter 9 treats the plug as this module's most serious failure.<br>• <b>Not optional at any size</b> — the two-person business and the hundred-branch network reconcile for the same reasons, and the cost — about an hour per bank per month at a healthy business — is the cheapest error and fraud detection available.</p><p><b>What the unreconciled business cannot know.</b> Whether its cash position is real (decisions made on a GL balance the bank disagrees with are decisions made on fiction). Whether payments landed (the transfer that failed silently; the cheque never presented). What the bank has been taking (charges and deductions accumulating unbooked). And whether anyone is helping themselves — the classic frauds (the intercepted payment, the altered beneficiary, the takings banked short) are all caught first at reconciliation, or not at all.</p><p><b>Module contents.</b> Chapter 2: the setup — bank accounts and payment modes. Chapter 3: the matching routine. Chapter 4: working the differences. Chapter 5: cash — the float and the imprest system. Chapter 6: the retail cash-up chain, from drawer to statement. Chapter 7: bank governance. Chapter 8: HealthTrade's banking year. Chapter 9: review and the law.</p>"),
L("Chapter 2 — Bank accounts and payment modes", "12", "<p>Reconciliation needs the wiring right first: the real-world banks represented so the machinery can see them.</p><p><b>One real account, one leaf.</b> Every actual bank account — each with its own statement — is its own <b>Bank-type ledger account</b>:</p><p>• Typed Bank, so the reconciliation machinery can see it (module 1's type rule).<br>• Named plainly for the long term: <i>GTB Current - 0123</i>, not <i>Bank 2</i>.<br>• Grouped under Bank Accounts for the rollup read.<br>• Paired with its <b>bank account record</b> — the master carrying the account number, branch, and reconciliation linkage — created together with the leaf.</p><p>Two refusals follow from the rule: never pool two real accounts into one leaf (two statements cannot reconcile against one ledger), and never split one real account across leaves.</p><p><b>Payment modes.</b> The Mode of Payment is the front-line vocabulary — Cash, Transfer, POS, Cheque — and each mode maps to the account the money truly lands in: Transfer to the receiving bank leaf, Cash to the cash account, POS to the settlement path Chapter 6 covers. A <b>mis-mapped mode</b> is the quiet disease this chapter exists to prevent: every payment taken on it posts to the wrong leaf, one bank's reconciliation inherits a stream of phantom entries and another's misses a stream of real ones — weeks of tangle from one dropdown. Verify the mode map at go-live, and re-walk it whenever banks change.</p><p><b>Movements between own accounts.</b> Cash banked, funds shifted between banks, the float replenished — these are <b>contra entries</b> (module 2's type): one leaf credited, the other debited, no income or expense anywhere; the business is moving money between its own pockets. The discipline is the pairing: a contra touches two reconcilable worlds — the sending statement and the receiving one (or the cash count) — and both sides must land. The transfer is recorded when initiated, matched on the sending statement when it leaves, matched on the receiving statement when it arrives, and the in-transit gap (initiated Friday, landed Monday) lives as a timing item both reconciliations can see. Chapter 4 ages it.</p><p><b>Multiple banks, deliberately.</b> Businesses hold several banks for real reasons — clearing relationships, lending, branch cash handling. Keep the purposes written: each account's job noted on its record (operating, tax reserve, payroll), so the account list stays a design rather than an accumulation, and the annual review can ask whether each account still earns its place.</p><p><b>Summary.</b> One real account per typed leaf, plainly named, paired with its record; every payment mode mapped to its true landing account and verified at go-live; own-account movements as contras with both sides matched; and each bank's purpose written down.</p>"),
L("Chapter 3 — The reconciliation routine", "13", "<p>The reconciliation itself: monthly, per account, from the statement's own cutoff date.</p><p><b>The frame.</b> Two lists face each other: the statement's lines for the period, and the GL bank account's entries. The goal is total mutual accounting — every statement line matched to a ledger entry, every ledger entry matched to a statement line — with the two closing balances tied together through the <b>explained</b> remainder. The classic arithmetic: <b>GL balance + deposits the bank has not yet seen − payments the bank has not yet cleared = statement balance.</b> When that equation closes with every remaining item named, the account is reconciled. When it closes only through an unexplained difference, it is not reconciled — it is approximately narrated, which is a different thing.</p><p><b>The matching pass.</b> Run from easiest to hardest:</p><p>1. <b>Exact amount-and-date pairs</b> — the bulk: transfers and deposits that behaved.<br>2. <b>Amount matches with date drift</b> — the clearance lag: the payment entered Tuesday appears on the statement Thursday. Match them, and record the <b>clearance date</b> on the ledger entry — the field that marks an entry as witnessed, and the anchor for every uncleared-items report afterwards.<br>3. <b>Composites</b> — one statement line covering several ledger entries (the batched deposit), or one ledger entry split across several statement lines (the split settlement).<br>4. <b>The remainder</b> — whatever stays unmatched on either side. This is not a failure; it is the routine's actual product: the worklist Chapter 4 takes over.</p><p><b>Naming the remainder.</b> Every surviving item belongs to one of three categories:</p><p>• <b>Timing items (business side)</b> — recorded by the business, not yet seen by the bank: the cheque issued and unpresented, the transfer in transit, the deposit lodged after the cutoff. Legitimate and expected — and aged (Chapter 4): a timing item that stops being timely stops being a timing item.<br>• <b>Bank-side items, unbooked</b> — seen by the bank, not yet recorded by the business: charges, interest, direct debits, standing orders, reversals, the customer who paid straight into the account unannounced. The reconciliation is where the business learns of these, and Chapter 4 books them from here — never from memory.<br>• <b>Errors</b> — either side's: the business's mis-keyed amount (corrected by module 2's rules), or — rare but real — the bank's own error, queried with the bank and tracked until reversed. Never absorbed.</p><p><b>Practical rules.</b> Reconcile from the statement's cutoff, not a convenient date. Work one account at a time to completion. And finish every session with the remainder list written: item, category, amount, age, owner. A reconciliation whose output is that list has done its job; one whose output is a tick has done nothing.</p>"),
L("Chapter 4 — Working the differences", "13", "<p>The remainder is named; now it is worked — each category's handling, plus the aging discipline that stops the reconciliation becoming a museum of old differences.</p><p><b>Timing items: ride, then investigate.</b> The unpresented cheque and the in-transit transfer ride to next month's reconciliation, where most clear. The discipline is the <b>age limit</b>, written per instrument:</p><p>• <b>Cheques</b> — the validity window. A cheque unpresented at six months is stale by banking practice and must be resolved, not carried: contact the payee; if the cheque was lost or never received, reverse its ledger entry by the correction rules (with references) and reissue the payment properly.<br>• <b>Transfers</b> — days, not months. A transfer in transit beyond a week is not in transit; it is missing. Trace it with the bank; find or force the reversal.<br>• <b>Deposits</b> — the loudest alarm in the module: a lodged deposit missing from the statement is escalated <b>same day</b>. Chapter 6's chain names who carried it.</p><p>The <b>uncleared-items report</b>, aged, is the reconciliation's standing output — and a remainder list that only grows is Chapter 9's pattern in its early form.</p><p><b>Bank-side items: book from the statement.</b> Charges, interest, direct debits, reversals — booked as Bank Entries, from the reconciliation, with the statement line referenced. That is the only lawful source: the bank account moves because the bank moved it, and the statement is the evidence. Two disciplines ride along:</p><p>• <b>Reasonableness.</b> Charges are read, not just booked — the charge schedule against the bank mandate. Banks err, and the business that books blindly subsidises the errors. A quarterly charges read against expectation is a small routine with a real yield.<br>• <b>The unexplained credit.</b> Money arriving unannounced is not income until identified. Park it as identified-pending, run the party investigation — it is usually a customer paying without a reference, resolved through the receivables machinery so the allocation lands properly. Never swallow it into income out of relief.</p><p><b>Errors: the correction rules, applied.</b> Business-side errors surface here constantly — the transposed amount, the payment posted to the wrong bank leaf, the double entry. Every one corrects by module 2's rules: reverse and repost with references and remarks; and where a document erred, correct the document. The reconciliation's job is finding them; the correction rules' job is fixing them without rewriting history.</p><p><b>Summary.</b> Timing items ride under written age limits and are investigated at the limit — cheque by its validity, transfer by the week, missing deposit same-day. Bank-side items book only from the statement, with a quarterly reasonableness read and unexplained credits parked, never pocketed. Errors correct by the rules. The aged remainder list is the standing output, and its length and age are the reconciliation's quality gauge.</p>"),
L("Chapter 5 — Cash: the float and the imprest system", "12", "<p>The statement witnesses electronic money. Physical cash has no outside witness at all — which is why its rules are stricter and older.</p><p><b>Cash accounts: few, and owned.</b> A Cash-type leaf for each real cash <b>custody</b> — the main cash account, each petty cash float — never one diffuse Cash account that several drawers share. Pooled custody is no custody: when several people share one cash balance, no variance can be attributed to anyone. Each custody has a named holder, a counting rhythm, and a written purpose. And the design goal is <b>minimisation</b>: cash custodies are attack surface, and Nigeria's transfer-heavy payment reality is an ally — fewer drawers, smaller floats, faster banking, less to defend.</p><p><b>The imprest float.</b> Petty cash runs on the imprest system — the design that makes small cash self-controlling:</p><p>1. A <b>fixed float</b> (say ₦100,000) held by a named custodian.<br>2. Every disbursement against a <b>voucher</b> — what, why, who, with the receipt attached.<br>3. <b>Replenishment reimburses exactly the vouchers' total</b>, restoring the float to its fixed level.</p><p>The invariant that makes it work: <b>cash in the box plus vouchers in the box equals the float, at every moment.</b> A spot count takes two minutes and either ties or tells. Replenishment is where the accounting happens — the vouchers booked to their expense accounts, by dimension, in one entry, with the reimbursement transfer as the bank-side event. The GL stays clean of dribbles while every kobo stays evidenced.</p><p><b>The cash count.</b> Scheduled counts (the float monthly; tills daily — Chapter 6) plus <b>unscheduled spot counts</b> — unpredictability is what keeps a counting programme honest. Counted by someone other than the custodian where the roster allows, against the invariant, with the standard variance rules: named the same day, investigated before adjusted, reason-coded, and read as a rate per custody over time. And the framing that matters for the people: <b>the count protects the honest holder</b> — the clean-count record is the custodian's own evidence. A custodian who welcomes spot counts is telling you something, as is one who resents them.</p><p><b>Cash rules, itemised.</b></p><p>• Disbursements only against vouchers — no IOUs living in the box; an IOU is an unauthorised loan wearing a scrap of paper.<br>• The float is fixed — topping it up by feel breaks the invariant.<br>• A spending cap routes anything substantial to the proper payment machinery, where it gets evidence and approval.<br>• Custodian changes are a counted handover: outgoing count, incoming count, both signed, same day.</p>"),
L("Chapter 6 — The cash-up chain: drawer to statement", "13", "<p>Retail's daily money routine — the chain from the till drawer to the bank statement — is where more small money is lost than anywhere else in trade. The chain has four links, and each one is signed.</p><p><b>Link 1 — the till session.</b> Each till, each day or shift, is a session: opened with a counted float, traded through the till's machinery (every sale a document), closed with a counted drawer. The session is the accountability unit — one till, one operator, one reconciliation. A pooled till (several operators, one drawer) converts every variance into an argument, which is why it is refused.</p><p><b>Link 2 — the cash-up.</b> At session close:</p><p>1. The drawer is counted <b>by denomination</b> — the count sheet by note, because <i>roughly ₦180k</i> is not a count.<br>2. The count is compared against the system's session totals <b>by mode</b>: cash sales against cash in the drawer (less the float); POS-terminal sales against the terminal's settlement report; transfers against the transfer log.<br>3. Variances are named <b>tonight</b>, same day, while memory serves. A ₦14,000 short investigated at close is a training catch; discovered a week later it is an argument; a month later, a write-off.</p><p>Cash-up variances are read as a rate per till and per operator — rotating operators across tills separates the till from the person where a pattern appears — and the clean-session streak is the operator's own visible record.</p><p><b>Link 3 — banking the takings.</b> Cash does not sleep in drawers:</p><p>• <b>Banked intact</b> — the whole count, never the count minus informal spendings. Takings that detour into expenses break the tie between till total and deposit slip, and every informal spend is an undocumented one.<br>• <b>Banked fast</b> — daily or next morning per policy and geography, the overnight safe's contents minimal.<br>• The deposit: counted, sealed, slipped, carried by a <b>named person</b>, lodged — and the deposit slip's amount must equal the cash-up's banked figure to the naira, because the next link checks it.</p><p><b>Link 4 — the statement closes the loop.</b> The deposit appears on the bank statement, and the reconciliation (Chapter 3) matches it. The full chain: <b>till total → cash-up → deposit slip → statement line</b> — one unbroken sequence of agreeing numbers, each link signed by its custodian. When the chain breaks, the break names its link: a till-to-cash-up gap is a session problem; a cash-up-to-slip gap happened in counting or carriage; a slip-to-statement gap is the same-day alarm from Chapter 4.</p><p><b>Summary.</b> One operator per session; denominated counts compared by mode, variances named tonight; takings banked intact and fast by named carriage; the statement matched at reconciliation. Four links, four signatures, one agreeing number end to end.</p>"),
L("Chapter 7 — Bank governance", "12", "<p>Stock walks out in cartons; bank money leaves at wire speed. This chapter assembles the controls that defend it.</p><p><b>The release separation.</b> The <b>preparer</b> of a payment — who assembles what to pay from system evidence — is never its <b>releaser</b> — who authorises it at the bank. And the payment run pays what the ledger supports: approved invoices, matched documents — never what a message asks. The separation survives every size of business because it is two people, not two departments: at eleven people, the founder releases what the accountant prepares.</p><p><b>Masters, not messages.</b> The beneficiary-fraud defence:</p><p>• Payments go to the account details in the <b>supplier master</b> — never to details supplied in a message.<br>• Changes to those details are a governed master-data event: verified through a known channel back to a known person — never the phone number in the requesting email.<br>• The urgent message asking otherwise — the MD's spoofed mailbox, the supplier's lookalike domain, the WhatsApp that sounds exactly right — dies against the process. That is the point of having one.</p><p>The accounts team is this fraud's chosen target, because they hold the pen that pays. Refresh the training annually, and treat reported near-misses as wins, not embarrassments.</p><p><b>Mandates and signatories.</b> The bank-side mirror of the system's permissions: who may sign, at what thresholds, in what combinations — kept <b>aligned</b> with the internal release design. A mandate broader than the internal control is a back door; one narrower is a bottleneck. Review the mandate on the annual walk <b>and at every personnel change</b>: the departed signatory is removed the week they leave, not the year — a stale mandate is a standing invitation. Online banking gets the same treatment in modern clothes: users named, limits set, tokens held personally, and the admin rights themselves held by two named people.</p><p><b>The bank calendar.</b> The recurring lines this module adds:</p><p>• <b>Monthly:</b> the reconciliation, per account — with a written maximum tolerable unreconciled age (an account two months unreconciled is an incident, not a backlog); the uncleared-items aging read.<br>• <b>Quarterly:</b> the charges reasonableness read.<br>• <b>Annually and on change:</b> the mandate and online-user review.<br>• <b>Per their schedules:</b> the cash counts (Chapter 5) and till sessions (Chapter 6).<br>• <b>Daily, where cash flow is tight:</b> the cash position read across the banks.</p><p><b>Summary.</b> Prepare and release separated; beneficiaries paid from masters with governed changes; mandates aligned, reviewed, and purged the week people leave; and the calendar — monthly reconciliation with a maximum age, quarterly charges read, annual mandate walk.</p>"),
L("Chapter 8 — Case study: HealthTrade's banking year", "13", "<p>The outside witness, installed and lived. Every chapter appears.</p><p><b>The wiring and the first reconciliation (Chapters 2-4).</b> Three banks become three typed leaves with written purposes — operating, tax reserve, payroll — and the mode map is verified (module 1's go-live check had already caught one mis-mapping). Then the hardest hour of the whole setup: the <b>first reconciliation</b> of the operating account, facing eighteen months of never-reconciled history. The dig, run once, properly: the matching pass leaves a remainder of <b>34 items</b>, and worked by category it resolves into:</p><p>• A nine-month-old <b>stale cheque</b> — ₦210,000 to a supplier who never presented it. The payee is contacted (the cheque was lost), the ledger entry reversed by the correction rules with references, and the payment reissued through the proper run.<br>• An unbooked <b>charges trail</b> — fourteen months of charges and levies never entered, booked in one referenced sitting. The reasonableness read then finds <b>two duplicate charges</b>, queried and recovered from the bank: ₦38,000 back.<br>• One <b>mis-mapped POS week</b> — settlements posted to the wrong bank leaf during a terminal change. Corrected by the rules; the mode map fixed.<br>• An <b>unexplained credit</b> of ₦127,000 — parked identified-pending, traced within the week to a customer who paid without a reference, and allocated through receivables so the tie held.</p><p>Month two's reconciliation takes fifty minutes. Month three's, forty.</p><p><b>The float installed (Chapter 5).</b> The office's drawer-of-mystery becomes a ₦100,000 imprest float: named custodian, voucher pad, the invariant taught — <i>cash plus vouchers equals one hundred, always</i> — and monthly replenishment through the payment machinery with vouchers booked by dimension. The first unscheduled spot count, week three, ties to the naira, and the custodian asks when the next one is. <i>Unscheduled</i>, comes the answer — the system working.</p><p><b>The cash-up chain (Chapter 6).</b> Till sessions start at the retail counter: opening floats, denominated closing counts, mode-split cash-up. Day nine catches the chain's first fish: the evening count is <b>₦14,000 short</b> against cash sales — named that night, investigated while memory serves, and resolved as two transfer-paid sales rung as cash by a new operator. A mode error, not a theft: the sessions are corrected by document, the operator trained, and the variance rate returns to zero. Takings bank intact next morning by named carriage; the slips match the cash-ups to the naira; the statement matches the slips — the chain closes its loop for the first full month.</p><p><b>Governance (Chapter 7).</b> The mandate review finds a former manager still a signatory eight months after leaving — removed the same week, and the personnel-change trigger added to the leaver checklist. The prepare/release separation is confirmed live: the accountant prepares the run; the MD releases it. The year ends with three banks reconciling in under two hours total per month, and the MD's note: <i>I used to check the bank app and hope. Now the books and the bank argue it out monthly, and I read the result.</i></p>"),
L("Chapter 9 — Common mistakes & the third law", "11", "<p>The banking layer's recurring failures, then the review list.</p><p><b>Pattern 1 — the never-reconciled account.</b> Symptom: a GL bank balance nobody would bet on; decisions made on it anyway; the statement opened only to see if a payment landed. Cause: the outside record never examined (Chapters 1, 3). Fix: the first-dig reconciliation — priced honestly, it faces the whole history once — then the monthly rhythm, with the maximum-unreconciled-age policy making relapse an incident.</p><p><b>Pattern 2 — reconciliation by plug.</b> Symptom: accounts that reconcile perfectly every month via a balancing journal to <i>reconciliation differences</i> or suspense. Cause: the module's most serious failure — forcing agreement converts every real difference (error, fraud, drift) into a booked fiction, and the account looks reconciled precisely because it never is. Fix: excavate the plug account's history (each plugged amount is an unworked difference), forbid the plug, and install the name-every-item discipline. An unexplained difference carried openly is honest; one booked away is not.</p><p><b>Pattern 3 — the museum remainder.</b> Symptom: uncleared items riding for a year; stale cheques immortal; in-transit items old enough to walk. Cause: the aging discipline absent (Chapter 4). Fix: age limits written per instrument, the monthly aging read with investigation at the limit, and the remainder list's length and age on the close checklist as the reconciliation's quality gauge.</p><p><b>Pattern 4 — cash counted never.</b> Symptom: the drawer of mystery; IOUs in the float; petty cash topped up by feel; variances discovered annually and absorbed silently. Cause: the no-witness asset given no discipline either (Chapter 5). Fix: the imprest installed (the invariant makes counting trivial), custodies named and minimal, the count rhythm with spot checks, and the spending cap routing real expenses to the evidenced machinery.</p><p><b>Pattern 5 — the leaking chain.</b> Symptom: till totals, cash-ups, deposit slips and statement lines each telling a slightly different number; takings banked late and light; variance arguments a week after the fact. Cause: the chain's links unsigned (Chapter 6) — pooled tills, undenominated counts, informal spendings en route, unnamed carriage. Fix: sessions and same-day cash-ups, intact and fast banking by named carriage, and the four-link match run monthly at reconciliation.</p><p><b>The review list.</b> Confirm you can state: why the outside record is categorically stronger; the wiring rules (one account one leaf, the mode map, contras); the matching order and the reconciliation equation; the three remainder categories and each one's handling with its age limit; the imprest invariant and the cash rules; the four links of the cash-up chain and what each break means; and the governance set — prepare/release, masters-not-messages, mandates aligned and purged.</p><p><b>The third law: every bank account is reconciled monthly, and every difference is named — never plugged.</b> Module 4 turns to the promises: receivables and payables from the accounts chair.</p>"),
]

