# ============================ ACCOUNTS 4 ============================
LESSONS_A4 = [
L("Chapter 1 — The accounts chair: owning the record", "11", "<p>The sales desk runs credit control and collections; the purchasing desk runs the invoice match and the payment run. This module sits in a third chair — the <b>accounts chair</b> — and its distinction is one sentence: <b>the trading desks own the relationships; the accounts chair owns the record</b> — its integrity, its honesty, and what it asserts to everyone who reads the balance sheet.</p><p><b>The two promise lines.</b> Debtors and Creditors are the balance sheet's promise accounts: money the world owes the business, and money the business owes the world. They are usually the largest figures after stock — and unlike stock, which can be physically counted, a receivable is only as real as its record. No shelf holds it; its entire existence is the party's ledger, the documents behind it, and the tie that proves the total. Someone must own that bookkeeping with the same rigor the warehouse owns the shelves. This module is that ownership, made operational.</p><p><b>The chair's first duty: the tie.</b> Module 1 built the control-to-party tie; this module operates it: checked monthly (control balance against the party report's sum, to the kobo — Chapter 7 schedules it), divergences treated as incidents with the journal filter as the first tool. Every discipline in the chapters ahead is tie maintenance in some form: allocation hygiene keeps the details telling their story cleanly (Chapter 2), provisioning keeps the total honest about collectability (Chapter 4), confirmations test the record against the world (Chapter 5), and migration decides whether the tie is born intact or born broken (Chapter 6).</p><p><b>The four questions.</b> The trading desks read these balances operationally — who to chase, who to pay. The accounts chair reads them as assertions. The balance sheet says ₦18.4m is receivable. Is it:</p><p>• <b>Real</b> — every naira party-attached and document-backed?<br>• <b>Collectable</b> — the provision question: ₦18.4m gross of which ₦1.2m is doubtful is a different assertion.<br>• <b>Current</b> — the aging honest, advances not netted against debts (Chapter 3's rule)?<br>• <b>Complete</b> — nothing quietly deleted, nothing parked off-ledger?</p><p>Four adjectives — <b>real, collectable, current, complete</b> — and an auditor's entire receivables programme is those four questions asked systematically. A chair that keeps them answered all year meets any audit calmly.</p><p><b>Module contents.</b> Chapter 2: allocation hygiene. Chapter 3: advances, both directions. Chapter 4: provisioning and bad debts. Chapter 5: statements and confirmations. Chapter 6: opening balances and migration. Chapter 7: the month-end ritual. Chapter 8: HealthTrade's year in the chair. Chapter 9: review and the law.</p>"),
L("Chapter 2 — Allocation hygiene: the unallocated read", "12", "<p>Allocation is taught at the payment desks; the accounts chair inherits what the desks leave behind. This chapter is the standing cleanup that keeps party ledgers legible.</p><p><b>What unallocated means.</b> A party's ledger is entries; a party's <b>story</b> is entries matched — this payment against these invoices, this credit note applied to that invoice, this advance consumed by that bill. Every unmatched item is a naira of ambiguity:</p><p>• <b>The payment-on-account</b> sitting against no invoice: which debts does it settle? Until someone says, the aging lies — the invoices it should have cleared still age as unpaid while the ledger's net looks fine.<br>• <b>The credit note issued and never applied:</b> the customer's statement shows a debt and a floating credit side by side — and the customer, reasonably, pays the net and disputes the rest.<br>• <b>The advance unmatched</b> — Chapter 3's whole subject.</p><p>The balance can be right while the story is illegible, and illegible stories rot statements, aging reports, collections priorities, and eventually the relationship — because every conversation starts with twenty minutes of archaeology.</p><p><b>The unallocated read.</b> The standing report: unallocated payments, unapplied credit notes, unmatched advances — per party, aged, valued. Read monthly (Chapter 7 schedules it) and worked to near-zero. Each item is:</p><p>1. <b>Matched</b> — allocated against its invoices, driven from the accounts side; where the intent is genuinely unknown, the customer is asked: <i>your payment of the 14th — against which invoices?</i> A professional question that also quietly tells the customer their account is watched.<br>2. <b>Refunded</b> — the genuine overpayment returned through the proper payment machinery.<br>3. <b>Escalated</b> — the item nobody can explain after honest work. Rare, and exactly what the chair exists to chase down rather than let ride.</p><p><b>Why near-zero compounds.</b> Kept near-zero: every statement is self-explanatory, every aging line is a real debt, every collections call starts from agreed facts, and the monthly tie check is trivial. Lapsed for two quarters: the aging overstates (unallocated credits hide inside net balances), collections chase already-paid debts (the relationship cost), statements teach customers to dispute (the training cost), and the cleanup becomes a project instead of a routine.</p><p>Practical rule: grade each month's allocation discipline by the list's length. A growing unallocated report is the earliest warning this module gives, and it costs one glance to read.</p>"),
L("Chapter 3 — Advances, both directions", "13", "<p>Money moves ahead of documents in both directions — customers pay before invoices; the business pays suppliers before bills. Advances are where promise accounting gets subtle, and both directions run on one discipline.</p><p><b>Customer advances: the liability.</b> Cash received ahead of earning is not income, and not a negative receivable left to confuse the aging — it is a <b>liability</b>: the business owes goods, services, or the money back. The handling:</p><p>1. <b>Captured as an advance</b> — the payment entry flagged, the liability posting where the configuration routes it.<br>2. <b>Matched to its invoice</b> when the trade completes — the advance consumed, the liability extinguished, the invoice settled in one motion.<br>3. <b>Reported as what it is</b> — the never-net rule below.</p><p>The advance left unmatched after its trade completed is Chapter 2's problem in its most expensive form: the customer who paid ahead <b>and</b> got invoiced now shows a debt and a credit nobody joined — and pays neither attention.</p><p><b>Supplier advances: the asset, and the known black hole.</b> The advance paid to a supplier is an <b>asset</b> (a prepayment — the business owns a claim), matched against the supplier's invoice when it lands, and <b>aged while it waits</b>. The advances-aging report — per supplier, valued, dated against expected delivery — is the chair's standing lamp over the classic failure: the advance paid, the goods arriving, and the bill settled <b>again</b> because nobody matched. Two structural defences:</p><p>• An advance aging past its expected delivery is either a late supplier (an expediting conversation) or a lost claim compounding quietly — the monthly aging decides which.<br>• The invoice-entry step <b>checks for open advances before settlement</b> — a workflow habit the chair audits, because the advance the payment run does not know about is the one that gets paid twice.</p><p><b>The reporting rule: never net.</b> The subtle sin this chapter forbids: netting — a customer's advance netted against debts into one rolled figure, or advances buried inside the receivables line so the balance sheet shows ₦18.4m receivable when the truth is ₦19.6m owed-to-us and ₦1.2m owed-by-us. The two directions are different assertions (an asset and a liability), different risks, and different tax stories. The statements show them separately, always. The reader's test: can a banker see the real position from the face of the statement?</p><p><b>Summary.</b> Customer advances: liability at capture, consumed at invoice, never left floating. Supplier advances: asset at capture, aged against expected delivery, checked before every settlement. Both directions: anchored to expected dates, reviewed monthly, and never netted in reporting.</p>"),
L("Chapter 4 — Provisioning and bad debts", "13", "<p>Some promises break. The chair's hardest duty is saying so — in the books, on a policy, before anyone asks. The doctrine: doubtful, provided, written off, and sometimes recovered.</p><p><b>The two stages.</b></p><p>• <b>Doubtful.</b> Collection is uncertain — the customer silent past every escalation, disputing without engaging, visibly distressed. Response: a <b>provision</b> — an estimate of the loss, charged now: DR bad-debt expense, CR provision for doubtful debts (a contra-asset sitting against gross receivables). The debt itself <b>stays on the party's ledger</b>, and collections keeps working it.<br>• <b>Bad.</b> Collection has failed definitively — the customer wound up, vanished, or pursuit costs more than the claim per policy. Response: the <b>write-off</b> — the debt removed from the party ledger against the provision (or straight to expense where unprovided), governed like every destruction of value: evidence documented (the chase history), approvals by threshold (two names, value-routed), the tax notes captured (deductibility has conditions — the accountant owns them), and <b>the party's record retained</b>: written off is not erased; the history survives for the day the customer returns wanting credit.</p><p><b>The provision policy: a matrix, not a mood.</b> The provision's size is an estimate, and estimates need policy or they become tuning knobs. The standard instrument is the <b>aging matrix</b> — provision percentages by age band, for example: 0-60 days nil, 61-120 at 10%, 121-180 at 25%, 181-365 at 50%, over a year at 100%. The numbers are the accountant's policy call, informed by the business's actual collection history; the structure is universal. Two adjustments ride on top:</p><p>• The large debt with specific bad news is provided specifically, regardless of age.<br>• The aged debt with a signed payment plan performing on schedule can be relieved judgmentally — the matrix is the floor; judgment documents its exceptions.</p><p><b>The mechanics each period.</b> The matrix is applied to the fresh aging; the entry adjusts the provision <b>to</b> the required level — up as a charge, down as a release — and the <b>movement</b>, not the balance, is what the P&amp;L reader sees. This is why the policy must be stable: a provision moved by policy is information (collections got worse, or better); one moved by discretion is noise at best.</p><p><b>Recoveries.</b> A written-off debt sometimes pays. The recovery lands as income through the payment machinery, referenced end to end — and it lands cleanly precisely because the party's record survived the write-off. Chapter 8 shows HealthTrade's own case.</p><p><b>Summary.</b> Doubtful → provision by matrix, debt stays and is chased. Bad → write-off on two signatures with evidence, record retained. Policy stable, movements explained, exceptions documented, recoveries through the machinery.</p>"),
L("Chapter 5 — Statements and confirmations", "12", "<p>The party ledger faces the world through four routines: the statement run, on-record dispute handling, the audit confirmation, and the supplier-statement reconciliation.</p><p><b>The statement run.</b> The customer statement <b>is</b> the party ledger, printed: monthly, to every open-balance customer, showing the period's documents, payments, allocations, and the aged balance. The run is cheap — the machinery formats what allocation hygiene keeps legible — and it returns three things:</p><p>1. <b>Collections pressure</b> — the statement is the chase's ambient reminder.<br>2. <b>Free error-surfacing</b> — customers audit their statements: the missed payment, the unapplied credit, the invoice they never received all come back as queries, and each one is tie maintenance done for you.<br>3. <b>Dispute hygiene</b> — disagreements handled <b>on record</b>: the disputed line flagged in the system, the resolution documented, and the correcting instrument (credit note, allocation, or a firm restatement of the debt) issued as a document. Never as side-agreements living in someone's inbox — the side-agreement is the statement's silent contradiction and an audit's favourite finding.</p><p><b>The audit confirmation.</b> The external audit's receivables centrepiece: the auditor selects parties, sends balance confirmations, and compares the replies to the ledger. A chair that ran this module's disciplines meets it calmly — balances party-attached and document-backed (real), provisions on policy (collectable), advances un-netted (current), nothing deleted (complete). The four adjectives, pre-answered. One courtesy helps: warn the selected customers that confirmations are coming — an expected confirmation gets a reply; an ignored one costs a follow-up cycle.</p><p><b>The mirror: supplier statement reconciliation.</b> The payables side has its own near-outside witness: the <b>supplier's statement</b> of your account, reconciled against your creditor ledger. Not as independent as the bank — the supplier writes it, and suppliers err in both directions — but structurally the same routine: their lines matched to your entries, the remainder named. The catches it makes:</p><p>• <b>Invoices they show that you never received</b> — the missing-bill catch: a liability that exists in the world but not in your books, found before it arrives as a surprise demand.<br>• <b>Credit notes they issued that you never captured</b> — money owed to you, sitting unclaimed.<br>• <b>Payments you made that they never applied</b> — the allocation conversation, from the other side.</p><p>Run it monthly for the top suppliers, on rotation for the rest (Chapter 7 schedules it).</p><p><b>Summary.</b> Statements monthly to every open balance; disputes on record with document instruments; confirmations met with the four adjectives pre-answered; and the supplier statements reconciled — the mirror that catches missing bills and unclaimed credits.</p>"),
L("Chapter 6 — Opening balances and migration", "13", "<p>Every AR/AP ledger was born somewhere — usually in a migration weekend — and how it was born decides whether the chair inherits a working tie or an archaeology site.</p><p><b>The loading rule: detail, never blobs.</b> Opening receivables load <b>per party, per open document</b>: each customer's balance decomposed into its actual open invoices — numbers, dates, amounts, due dates from the old records — entered so that:</p><p>• The new system's <b>aging is true from day one</b> — every balance ages from its real invoice date, not from go-live.<br>• Every future payment has <b>real invoices to allocate against</b> — Chapter 2's hygiene is possible at all.<br>• The <b>tie is born intact</b> — the control equals the sum of real party detail.</p><p>The blob alternative — one opening balance per customer, or worst of all one journal to the Debtors control — is the tie born broken: a total with no details, an aging that starts blind (collections priorities scrambled for a year), and an allocation machinery with nothing to match (every payment on-account forever). The same rule mirrors to payables: per supplier, per open bill — the payment run's evidence and the supplier-statement reconciliation both need documents, not assertions.</p><p><b>The mechanics.</b></p><p>• Use the opening instruments the setup provides — opening invoice tools, or governed journals per party per document — posting against the opening equity account so the openings do not fabricate revenue or expense in year one.<br>• Date with the <b>true original dates</b> where the aging must be honest; where the system's opening pattern forces posting-date compromises, preserve the due dates — the accountant's call, documented.<br>• <b>Advances load as advances</b> — never netted into balances; Chapter 3's rule applies from day one.<br>• <b>Disputed balances load flagged</b> — the dispute history summarised, not laundered by migration.<br>• <b>Provisions load as the policy's opening position</b> — the matrix applied to the opening aging, so the honesty doctrine is born with the ledger rather than retrofitted at the first year-end.</p><p><b>The migration reconciliation.</b> The proof, before go-live is called done:</p><p>1. The new system's control balances tie to the old system's (or the signed trial balance's) totals <b>to the kobo</b>.<br>2. The new party sums tie to the old party listings.<br>3. The differences — there are always differences — are worked to zero or documented as deliberate corrections: the old system's known errors fixed at migration, listed, and signed off.</p><p>Practical rule: price the detailed load honestly at planning time. It costs days where the blob costs hours — and the blob's true price is a year of blind aging, permanent on-account payments, and a tie that never agreed. The detail is the cheap option; it just pays upfront.</p>"),
L("Chapter 7 — The month-end AR/AP ritual", "12", "<p>The chair's disciplines, assembled into one monthly sitting. Ninety minutes at a healthy business, one owner, one list — run in this order:</p><p><b>1. The tie check — first, always.</b> Debtors control against the customer balance sum; Creditors against the supplier sum; to the kobo. Agreement: one line, initialed, done — two minutes. Divergence: an <b>incident</b> — run the journal filter first (the answer is almost always a journal), find the offending entry, correct it by the rules, and never carry the divergence forward unexplained.</p><p><b>2. The unallocated sweep.</b> Chapter 2's read: unallocated payments, unapplied credits, per party, aged. Worked to near-zero; the escalations named. The month's allocation discipline is graded by this list's length.</p><p><b>3. The advance aging — both directions.</b> Customer advances against their expected completions; supplier advances against expected deliveries. Items aged past plausibility resolved or escalated; the never-net rule confirmed in the reporting.</p><p><b>4. The provision review.</b> The matrix run against the fresh aging; the required provision computed; the adjusting movement posted with its one-line explanation; specific judgments documented. Write-off candidates are <b>surfaced to their approval path</b> — the ritual nominates; the governance decides. Never both in the same sitting.</p><p><b>5. The statement run.</b> Customer statements out; last month's returned queries confirmed worked; disputes verified on-record.</p><p><b>6. Supplier statement reconciliations.</b> The month's tranche — top suppliers monthly, the rest on rotation. Remainders worked; missed credit notes captured; missing bills chased.</p><p><b>7. The outputs handed upward.</b> The ritual's products go to the close (module 7 receives them): the aged AR and AP with provision netting shown, the advance schedules, the tie confirmation, the write-off log, the unallocated residual. Each a one-page artifact — and each already true, because the ritual made it so.</p><p><b>The economics.</b> Ninety minutes monthly is the compounding investment: skipped for a quarter, every list doubles and the archaeology begins; kept, the year-end AR/AP work is a formality and the audit's receivables programme meets a record with the four adjectives pre-answered. The trading desks receive the ritual's feedback — the unallocated causes, the recurring deduction patterns — rather than attending the sitting.</p><p>Practical rule: the ritual has one owner — the accountant, or the credit controller wearing the accounts hat — and a fixed slot in the month. A ritual with a name and a date happens; one without either is a good intention.</p>"),
L("Chapter 8 — Case study: HealthTrade's year in the chair", "13", "<p>The accounts chair, installed and lived. Every chapter appears.</p><p><b>The sweep instituted (Chapter 2).</b> Month one's first unallocated read is the expected archaeology: <b>₦840,000 unallocated across 23 items</b> — payments-on-account going back nine months, four credit notes issued and never applied, two advances whose trades completed unmatched. Three afternoons of matching (customers called with the professional question — <i>your payment of March 14th, against which invoices?</i> — every call also quietly announcing the watched account), one genuine overpayment refunded properly, one escalation: a ₦60,000 payment no record explains, parked named, resolved in week three as a sister-company transfer mis-referenced. The read closes month two at ₦45,000, month three at near-zero — and the aging report, purged of hidden credits, promptly reveals <b>two genuinely overdue accounts</b> the old net balances had camouflaged. The sweep's first commercial dividend, handed to collections.</p><p><b>The provision policy built (Chapter 4).</b> The matrix is built from the business's own history — the last two years' collection outcomes by age band: nil to 60 days, 10% at 61-120, 30% at 121-180, 60% at 181-365, full past a year — written on the policy page and applied to the opening aging. The opening provision: <b>₦410,000</b>, charged with its explanation to the MD: <i>this is what the aging says the book is worth; the number was always true — now it is booked.</i> Monthly movements thereafter are small and explained, and Q3's release (collections improved) reads as exactly the good news it is, <b>because</b> the policy is stable enough for movements to mean something.</p><p><b>A write-off done right — and the surprise (Chapter 4).</b> Month five: the definitively-gone customer — premises vacated, phones dead, chase dossier complete — <b>₦188,000 written off</b> on the two-signature path, evidence filed, tax notes captured, the party record retained with its history. Month eleven: the surprise — the customer's principal resurfaces, restarts smaller, and pays <b>₦120,000</b> against the old debt for the relationship's sake. The recovery lands as income through the payment machinery, referenced end to end, and the party's history reads whole: debt, chase, write-off, recovery, new terms (cash-and-carry). The accountant's remark: <i>the record survived the write-off, so the recovery had somewhere to land.</i></p><p><b>The mirror's catch (Chapter 5).</b> The first supplier-statement reconciliation of the top five finds a missing bill — an invoice the supplier shows that never reached the books — captured before it arrived as a surprise demand, and a credit note from a returns batch that was never claimed: ₦74,000 recovered by asking. The monthly tranche joins the ritual.</p><p><b>The ritual settles (Chapter 7).</b> By month six the sitting runs at its promised ninety minutes: tie checked in two, the sweep short, advances aged, the matrix movement one line, statements out, the mirror's tranche done, seven one-page outputs to the close. The MD's year note: <i>the receivables number used to be an opinion. Now it is four answered questions.</i></p>"),
L("Chapter 9 — Common mistakes & the fourth law", "11", "<p>The chair's recurring failures, then the review list.</p><p><b>Pattern 1 — the diverged tie.</b> Symptom: the control disagrees with the party sum; nobody knows since when; reports drawn from whichever number suits. Cause: the tie never checked (Chapters 1, 7) — journals to controls, blob migrations, deletions, each divergence compounding undated. Fix: the incident treatment — the journal filter, the divergence dated by working backwards, the correction by the rules, and the monthly check that makes the next divergence a one-month problem instead of a three-year one.</p><p><b>Pattern 2 — the netting sin.</b> Symptom: receivables that shrink when advances arrive; statements customers dispute on sight; a balance sheet position nobody can decompose. Cause: the two directions crushed together (Chapter 3). Fix: advances captured and reported as what they are; the un-netting migration where the sin is historical; and the reader's test — can a banker see the real position from the face of the statement?</p><p><b>Pattern 3 — the perpetual gross.</b> Symptom: receivables at face value for years; aged debts immortal; the write-off unknown as an instrument. Cause: the honesty doctrine absent (Chapter 4) — collectability asserted, never assessed. Fix: the matrix built from the business's own history, the opening provision charged with its adult explanation, and the write-off path governed and used.</p><p><b>Pattern 4 — the silent ledger.</b> Symptom: statements never sent; confirmations dreaded; supplier statements filed unread; disputes living in inboxes. Cause: the record hiding from examination (Chapter 5) — always a symptom of the hygiene it would fail. Fix: the hygiene first (Chapters 2-4), then the routines switched on in order — statements, supplier reconciliations, then the confirmation posture.</p><p><b>Pattern 5 — the immortal advance.</b> Symptom: advances aging past every plausible delivery; the schedule nobody reviews; the supplier advance paid twice. Cause: advances anchored to no expected date, reviewed on no rhythm (Chapter 3). Fix: expected dates at capture, the monthly aging in the ritual, the resolution paths actually walked, and the payment run's open-advance check made structural.</p><p><b>Pattern 6 — the deleted debtor.</b> Symptom: customers who vanish from the ledger with their balances; a record that cannot answer for its own past. Cause: deletion used where the write-off doctrine belonged — completeness broken. Fix: the write-off as the only exit for a debt, the record retained always, and the completeness question standing in every review.</p><p><b>The review list.</b> Confirm you can state: the chair's distinction and the four questions; the unallocated read's three resolutions and why near-zero compounds; both advance directions, the double-payment defence, and the never-net rule; the two stages of the honesty doctrine, the matrix, and the recovery path; the statement run's three returns and the supplier-statement mirror; the detail-never-blobs loading rule and the migration reconciliation; and the seven-step ninety-minute ritual with its outputs.</p><p><b>The fourth law: the chair keeps the record real, collectable, current and complete.</b> Module 5 turns to the tax custodies and Nigerian compliance.</p>"),
]

